  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you write a divide and conquer algorithm, like Merge Sort or Binary Search, it solves a big problem by breaking it into smaller subproblems of the same kind, solving each one recursively, then combining the results. The running time of an algorithm like this is naturally described by a <strong>recurrence relation</strong>, an equation that defines T(n) in terms of T applied to a smaller input.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Solving a recurrence by hand, expanding it level by level and summing the work, works but is slow and easy to get wrong. The <strong>Master Theorem</strong> gives you a direct shortcut: for the specific, very common recurrence shape that divide and conquer algorithms produce, it tells you the answer immediately, no expansion required.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-problem-does-it-solve",
      "title": "1. What problem does it solve?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you write a divide-and-conquer algorithm (like Merge Sort), it calls itself recursively. You get a&nbsp;<strong>recurrence equation</strong>&nbsp;that looks scary. The Master Theorem gives you the answer in seconds no math needed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>Think of it this way:</strong>You split a big problem into smaller pieces, solve each piece, then combine the results. The Master Theorem tells you how long that whole process takes.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "The formula",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "T(n) = a · T(n/b) + f(n)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every divide-and-conquer algorithm fits this shape",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249035186-38915809.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249035186-38915809.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249035186-38915809.png",
          "alt": "Master theorem divide and conquer formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "The recursion tree what's really happening",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Imagine your problem as a tree. Each node = one call. Each level = one round of splitting. The Master Theorem compares the cost at each level as the tree grows.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249037092-321564054.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249037092-321564054.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249037092-321564054.png",
          "alt": "Master theorem recursion tree"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Here are the&nbsp;<strong>points form of the diagram explanation</strong>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<ul><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Root level:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;One problem of size&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ cost&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">T(n)</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Level 1:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;The problem splits into&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">a subproblems</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, each of size&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n / b</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ cost&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">a · T(n / b)</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Level 2:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Each of those splits again →&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">a² subproblems</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, each of size&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n / b²</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ cost&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">a² · T(n / b²)</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Level k:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">aᵏ subproblems</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, each of size&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n / bᵏ</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ cost&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">aᵏ · T(n / bᵏ)</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Leaf level:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;When the size becomes&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">1</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, we reach&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">base cases T(1)</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Total levels of the tree:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">log_b n</strong><br></li><li><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Total number of leaf nodes:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">a^(log_b n)</strong><br></li></ul>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Conclusion:</strong><br>This recursion tree represents the recurrence:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Where:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>a</strong>&nbsp;→ number of subproblems created",
            "<strong>b</strong>&nbsp;→ factor by which problem size reduces",
            "<strong>f(n)</strong>&nbsp;→ work done at the current level outside recursive calls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "The total cost =<strong>sum of work at every level</strong>. Each level has<strong>aᵏ sub-problems</strong>, each of size<strong>n/bᵏ</strong>. The Master Theorem compares this growing work vs the shrinking input",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-visual-intuition-for-the-3-cases",
      "title": "2. Visual intuition for the 3 cases",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To understand the three cases, imagine the&nbsp;<strong>recursion tree&nbsp;</strong>of the algorithm. Each level of the tree represents work done by recursive calls and the combining step. The key idea is to see&nbsp;<strong>which part of the tree contributes the most work&nbsp;</strong>the leaves, every level equally, or the root.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Case 1: Recursion dominates",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785249038643-599398346.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Condition</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "f(n)=O(nc)where&nbsp;c&lt;log⁡baf(n)=O(nc)where&nbsp;c&lt;logb​a",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Simple meaning",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The&nbsp;<strong>extra work at each level (f(n)) is small</strong>&nbsp;compared to the work created by recursive calls.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "What happens",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Recursive calls keep multiplying.",
            "Leaves of the recursion tree become very large.",
            "<strong>Leaves do most of the work.</strong>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Result",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "T(n)=Θ(nlog⁡ba)T(n)=Θ(nlogb​a)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Intuition",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“Most work happens deep in the tree.”",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Case 2: Equal work everywhere",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249040101-687412428.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249040101-687412428.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249040101-687412428.png",
          "alt": "Case 2: equal work everywhere"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Condition</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "f(n)=Θ(nlog⁡ba)f(n)=Θ(nlogb​a)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Simple meaning",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The&nbsp;<strong>combine work and recursive work grow at the same rate</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "What happens",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Each level of the recursion tree does&nbsp;<strong>the same amount of work</strong>.",
            "The tree has&nbsp;<strong>log n levels</strong>."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Result",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "T(n)=Θ(nlog⁡ba⋅log⁡n)T(n)=Θ(nlogb​a⋅logn)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Intuition",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“Every level contributes equally.”",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Case 3: Combining dominates",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249041630-330362008.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249041630-330362008.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785249041630-330362008.png",
          "alt": "Case 3: combining dominates"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Condition</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "f(n)=Ω(nc)where&nbsp;c&gt;log⁡baf(n)=Ω(nc)where&nbsp;c&gt;logb​a",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Simple meaning",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The&nbsp;<strong>combine step is very expensive</strong>&nbsp;compared to recursive calls.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "What happens",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Most work happens&nbsp;<strong>at the top of the tree</strong>.",
            "Lower levels contribute very little."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Result",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "T(n)=Θ(f(n))T(n)=Θ(f(n))",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Intuition",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“The root dominates the cost.”",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-how-to-apply-it-nbsp-step-by-step",
      "title": "3. How to apply it: &nbsp;step by step",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>1. Write the recurrence</strong><br>Write the recurrence in the form&nbsp;<strong>T(n) = a · T(n/b) + f(n)</strong>. Identify&nbsp;<strong>a</strong>&nbsp;(number of subproblems),&nbsp;<strong>b</strong>&nbsp;(size reduction factor), and&nbsp;<strong>f(n)</strong>&nbsp;(extra work).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>2. Compute log_b(a)</strong><br>Calculate&nbsp;<strong>log_b(a)</strong>. This tells how fast the recursive work grows.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>3. Find the exponent of f(n)</strong><br>Look at&nbsp;<strong>f(n)</strong>&nbsp;and express it as&nbsp;<strong>n^c</strong>&nbsp;to find the value of&nbsp;<strong>c</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>4. Compare the values</strong><br>Compare&nbsp;<strong>c</strong>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>log_b(a)</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "If&nbsp;<strong>c &lt; log_b(a)</strong>&nbsp;→ Case 1",
            "If&nbsp;<strong>c = log_b(a)</strong>&nbsp;→ Case 2",
            "If&nbsp;<strong>c &gt; log_b(a)</strong>&nbsp;→ Case 3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>5. Apply the formula</strong><br>Use the case formula to get&nbsp;<strong>T(n)</strong>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Case 1 →&nbsp;<strong>Θ(n^{log_b(a)})</strong>",
            "Case 2 →&nbsp;<strong>Θ(n^{log_b(a)} · log n)</strong>",
            "Case 3 →&nbsp;<strong>Θ(f(n))</strong>."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>A technical note on rigor:</strong> the simplified c &lt; log_b(a) and c &gt; log_b(a) comparisons used above are what you will use in practice and in interviews. The fully rigorous statement requires a polynomial gap, f(n) = O(n^(log_b(a) minus epsilon)) for Case 1 and f(n) = Omega(n^(log_b(a) plus epsilon)) for Case 3, for some epsilon &gt; 0, not just a strictly smaller or larger exponent. Case 3 also technically requires a regularity condition, a times f(n/b) is at most c times f(n) for some c &lt; 1 and large enough n, which holds for essentially every f(n) you will encounter in practice, like polynomials and polynomial times logarithms. If f(n) falls in a gap between the cases, for example it differs from n^log_b(a) by only a logarithmic factor, the Master Theorem simply does not apply and you need the Akra Bazzi method or a direct recursion tree analysis instead.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-quick-reference-cheat-sheet",
      "title": "4. Quick reference cheat sheet",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "items": [],
          "url": "",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "{\"headers\":[\"Algorithm\",\"Recurrence\",\"Time Complexity\"],\"rows\":[[\"Merge Sort\",\"2T(n/2) + n\",\"Θ(n log n)\"],[\"Binary Search\",\"T(n/2) + 1\",\"Θ(log n)\"],[\"Quick Sort (avg)\",\"2T(n/2) + n\",\"Θ(n log n)\"],[\"Strassen\",\"7T(n/2) + n<sup>2</sup>\",\"Θ(n<sup>2.81</sup>)\"],[\"Linear Search\",\"T(n − 1) + 1\",\"Not applicable\"]]}",
          "text": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Note on Quick Sort: its real average case recurrence involves an average over every possible pivot position, not a clean guaranteed half split. 2T(n/2) plus n is the standard simplified stand in used to get the right asymptotic answer, Theta(n log n), but it is an approximation of the real analysis, not a literal recurrence Quick Sort always satisfies.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Master Theorem</b> only works when the sub-problem size is<strong>n/b</strong>(division), NOT<strong>n-1</strong>(subtraction). For T(n) = T(n-1) + f(n), use substitution or recursion tree method instead.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
